==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|quysshyn}}, from later {{der|en|fro|coissin}} (modern {{m|fr|coussin}}), from {{der|en|VL.|*coxīnus||seat pad}}, derived from {{der|en|la|coxa||hip, thigh}} (with the suffix possibly after {{m+|la|pulvīnus||pillow}}), ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*koḱs-|t=joint, limb}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkʊʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cushion.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʊʃən|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}
[[Image:Cushion.jpg|right|thumb|A striped '''cushion''' on a bed]]

# {{senseid|en|Q10971235}}A soft mass of material stuffed into a cloth bag, used for comfort or support.
#* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Tremarn Case|passage=“There the cause of death was soon ascertained ; the victim of this daring outrage had been stabbed to death from ear to ear with a long, sharp instrument, in shape like an antique stiletto, which {{...}} was subsequently found under the '''cushions''' of the hansom.{{nb...}}”}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=12|passage=There were many wooden chairs for the bulk of his visitors, and two wicker armchairs with red cloth '''cushions''' for superior people. From the packing-cases had emerged some Indian clubs,{{nb...}}, and all these articles{{nb...}} made a scattered and untidy decoration that Mrs. Clough assiduously dusted and greatly cherished.}}
## {{lb|en|Commonwealth}} A [[throw pillow]].
## A thin, flat pad used on hard [[chair]]s and sometimes [[toilet seat]]s.
# Something acting as a cushion, especially to [[absorb]] a [[shock]] or [[impact]].
## A pad on which gilders cut gold leaf.
## A mass of steam in the end of the cylinder of a steam engine to receive the impact of the piston.
## {{senseid|en|snooker}} {{lb|en|sports|billiards|snooker|pool}} The [[lip]] around a table in [[cue]] sports which absorbs some of the impact of the billiard balls and bounces them back.
## The [[pillow]] used in making [[bone lace]].
## An [[engraver]]'s [[pad]].
## {{lb|en|historical}} The rubber of an electrical machine.
## {{lb|en|historical}} A pad supporting a woman's hair.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} a sufficient quantity of an intangible object (like points or minutes) to allow for some of those points, for example, to be lost without hurting one's chances for successfully completing an objective.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 3, 2011|author=Arindam Rej|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/15473004.stm Fulham 4-1 Wisla Krakow]
|passage=Wisla made a bright start to the second half and Fulham keeper Mark Schwarzer was twice called into action, first saving Gervasio Nunez's deflected 20-yard effort and then smothering Gargula's free-kick.<br>But Fulham soon had the '''cushion''' of a third goal after more outstanding build-up play.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=7 March 2021|work=The Guardian|author=David Hytner|title=Manchester United catch City cold as Fernandes and Shaw end winning run|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2021/mar/07/manchester-city-manchester-united-premier-league-match-report|passage=It will most likely not alter the destination of the league title, which still seems bound for City, who retain an 11-point '''cushion''' at the top}}
## {{lb|en|finance|countable|uncountable}} Money kept in reserve.
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 Feb 90|volume=17|number=29|journal=Gay Community News|page=8|author=Pam Mitchell;Valerie Miller|title=Skepticism, Steel Mills And Everyday Stories|text=The entry-level wages are abominable, still something like $15,000 a year. You can't possibly be a working class person and live on that in New York City. You need some kind of family "'''cushion'''." So what you'll find in publishing is middle class professionals, people who see material about working class people as "foreign."}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=Belverd Needles; Marian Powers|title=Financial Accounting: Media Enhanced|page=826
|passage=Interest coverage is important because it is an indicator of how much '''cushion''' a company has in making its interest payments.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Stijn Claessens; Kirsten Forbes|title=International Financial Contagion|page=85
|passage=If one of the banks has a significant enough '''cushion''' of capital and a strong enough balance sheet, then it would not experience a bank run, and the domino effect in panel A would not have occurred.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} The dancer in the [[cushion dance]] who currently holds the cushion, or the dance itself.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1668| title=The Praise of Folly |author=Desiderius Erasmus|translator=John Wilson | editor=Helen Mary Allen|year_published=1913|page=35| passage=But of these kind of second Courses I am the onely Cook; though yet those ordinary practises of our Feasts, as choosing a King, throwing Dice, drinking Healths, trouling it Round, dancing the '''Cushion''' and the like, were not invented by the seven Wise Men but my Self, and that too for the common pleasure of Mankind.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1836| title=The Violin|author=George Dubourg| page=21| passage=The young man advances to the fiddler, drops a penny in the pot, and gives it to one of his companions. '''Cushion''' then dances round the room, followed by the pot, and when they again reach the fiddler, the '''cushion''' says, in a sort of recitative, accompanied by the music, 'This dance it will no farther go.'}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1877| title=Observations on Popular Antiquities|author=John Brand; Henry Ellis | page=394| passage=In reference to a nuptial feast, the cushion dance at weddings is thus mentioned in the Apophthegms of King James, the Earl of Worcester and others (1658): “At last when the Masque was ended and Time had brought in the Supper, ''the '''Cushion''' led the Dance out of the Parlour into the Hall'';”}}

